  "label": "TMUA Specimen · Paper 1 · Question 17",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA Specimen · Paper 1 · Question 17 — Determine when a parameter quadratic has distinct real roots.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E"
  ],
  "correct": "D",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Use the discriminant",
      "body": "<p>Put the equation in standard quadratic form and calculate its discriminant. The question has already excluded a zero leading coefficient.</p>",
      "recap": "Two distinct real roots require a strictly positive discriminant.",
      "pitfall": "Move the constant term to the left before choosing its sign.",
      "pause": "Simplify and factor the discriminant.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l03",
          "reminder": "Two distinct real roots require a strictly positive discriminant."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Check when it is positive",
      "body": "<p>Use your factorisation to find the parameter values where the discriminant is zero or positive.</p>",
      "recap": "A square is nonnegative and is zero only when its base is zero.",
      "pitfall": "A repeated root is not two distinct roots; keep the original restriction on a.",
      "pause": "Decide which allowed parameter values remain.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l03",
          "reminder": "A square is nonnegative and is zero only when its base is zero."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Because the question already assumes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the discriminant criterion applies to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Its discriminant is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p>This is positive exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Thus, among the stated nonzero values of a, every value except <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> gives distinct real roots. The full domain is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0211D;</mi></mrow></math> excluding 0 and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>Answer D: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</strong></p>",
